WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.150
This is Episode 4 of the
Start series, the last one,

2
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.862
from the Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:05.862 --> 00:00:10.125
The first three episodes made the case
and walked through the first eight weeks.

4
00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:13.375
This one is about the meeting
where you ask for the go-ahead.

5
00:00:13.375 --> 00:00:18.085
It turns the case into one page: four
local numbers, a pitch shaped for each

6
00:00:18.085 --> 00:00:23.187
person at the table, one decision with an
owner and a date, and the roles that sign.

7
00:00:23.187 --> 00:00:27.276
The framing and the numbers are design
guidance, so adapt them freely.

8
00:00:27.276 --> 00:00:29.200
Let's start with the page.

9
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.113
The one-page ask has three parts.

10
00:00:35.113 --> 00:00:39.001
A short reminder of why now:
deadlines set outside the agency,

11
00:00:39.001 --> 00:00:42.424
and identity as the thing
everything else depends on.

12
00:00:42.424 --> 00:00:45.137
Then your own numbers,
which make the case local.

13
00:00:45.137 --> 00:00:48.678
Then one decision: approve
the four-week pre-flight,

14
00:00:48.678 --> 00:00:51.038
with a named owner and a start date.

15
00:00:51.038 --> 00:00:52.537
It's deliberately small.

16
00:00:52.537 --> 00:00:56.962
It needs no vendor choice, just an
inventory, an owner and a date.

17
00:00:56.962 --> 00:01:00.424
Everything after that is
decided at gates, from evidence.

18
00:01:00.424 --> 00:01:02.120
So, which numbers?

19
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.332
Four numbers do more than any slide.

20
00:01:08.332 --> 00:01:11.800
How many Windows Server
2019 servers you run,

21
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.983
since support ends in January 2029, once
you've confirmed no 2016 server remains.

22
00:01:17.983 --> 00:01:21.427
How many contractors sit
outside the HR system,

23
00:01:21.427 --> 00:01:24.695
because HR 2.0 covers
federal employees only.

24
00:01:24.695 --> 00:01:27.273
How long a departing person keeps access,

25
00:01:27.273 --> 00:01:31.520
from the day HR records the separation
to the last account disabled.

26
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.110
And your Federal HR 2.0 wave and dates,

27
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.895
because the chance to design identity in
closes when your HR connections go live.

28
00:01:39.895 --> 00:01:43.976
Once the pre-flight has run, you can
add one more from the machine side:

29
00:01:43.976 --> 00:01:46.745
how many service accounts
have no named owner.

30
00:01:46.745 --> 00:01:49.660
Each person at the table
hears these differently.

31
00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:58.160
For the CIO, lead with the clocks, and
the cost of meeting them piecemeal.

32
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.606
Server end-of-support dates,
the edge-device directive

33
00:02:01.606 --> 00:02:04.822
and the new logging plan
all land on the same estate.

34
00:02:04.822 --> 00:02:10.185
A set of separate projects can only race
those dates; one program can sequence them.

35
00:02:10.185 --> 00:02:13.735
The first step costs four
weeks and no vendor decision.

36
00:02:13.735 --> 00:02:18.229
Be straight about later costs: the
program has no license of its own,

37
00:02:18.229 --> 00:02:20.435
but the cloud services it uses do.

38
00:02:20.435 --> 00:02:25.010
And running past end of support becomes
a risk someone must formally accept.

39
00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.780
Next, the CISO.

40
00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.818
For the CISO, lead with leavers.

41
00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:36.404
When HR records a separation,
nothing else is listening.

42
00:02:36.404 --> 00:02:39.147
A ticket disables the
main account days later,

43
00:02:39.147 --> 00:02:43.043
other accounts stay live, and a
review months on finds a few of them.

44
00:02:43.043 --> 00:02:45.604
That's not carelessness; it's the wiring.

45
00:02:45.604 --> 00:02:48.718
It's also where account-management
findings come from,

46
00:02:48.718 --> 00:02:52.868
since the controls expect accounts
to follow termination and transfer.

47
00:02:52.868 --> 00:02:56.640
HR-driven identity fixes the
cause, starting with leavers,

48
00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.492
and measures the time from
separation to block from day one.

49
00:03:00.492 --> 00:03:03.060
That number is your before and after.

50
00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.047
For the chief human capital
officer, lead with timing.

51
00:03:10.047 --> 00:03:13.669
HR 2.0 moves the agency
onto one government-wide

52
00:03:13.669 --> 00:03:17.198
platform whether or not
identity rides along.

53
00:03:17.198 --> 00:03:20.930
During onboarding, the agency
designs its new connections,

54
00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.748
and that's the moment to
add the identity feed.

55
00:03:23.748 --> 00:03:27.348
Once those connections go live,
adding it means reopening them.

56
00:03:27.348 --> 00:03:29.434
The second point is contractors.

57
00:03:29.434 --> 00:03:34.523
HR 2.0 covers federal employees, so
contractors need a source of their own,

58
00:03:34.523 --> 00:03:37.660
and onboarding is the
right time to choose it.

59
00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.680
HR decides what the attributes mean;
identity decides how they flow.

60
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.455
For the authorizing
official, lead with evidence.

61
00:03:49.455 --> 00:03:54.343
They're the only official who can accept
risk for the agency, and every risk the

62
00:03:54.343 --> 00:03:59.230
program carries, rather than removes,
reaches them in writing, with an end date.

63
00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:02.418
In return, they get something
better than attestation.

64
00:04:02.418 --> 00:04:06.042
Every gate is checked from data,
every change is a signed plan,

65
00:04:06.042 --> 00:04:11.043
and evidence comes out of the work instead
of being assembled before an assessment.

66
00:04:11.043 --> 00:04:16.093
Automated changes stay gated, with a
person approving before anything runs.

67
00:04:16.093 --> 00:04:19.390
That's a posture an
authorizing official can sign.

68
00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.553
Write the decision as one
sentence someone can say yes to.

69
00:04:26.553 --> 00:04:30.628
Approve the four-week pre-flight,
sponsored by a named executive,

70
00:04:30.628 --> 00:04:33.397
run by a named steward,
starting on a set date,

71
00:04:33.397 --> 00:04:37.627
with the first gate on the calendar
beside the external deadlines.

72
00:04:37.627 --> 00:04:41.689
Before saying yes, a sponsor should
be able to answer a few questions.

73
00:04:41.689 --> 00:04:44.203
Is the source of truth
under version control?

74
00:04:44.203 --> 00:04:45.965
Can every change be traced?

75
00:04:45.965 --> 00:04:48.427
Does every decision
have one named decider?

76
00:04:48.427 --> 00:04:52.127
If the room hesitates on any
of those, that's useful too.

77
00:04:52.127 --> 00:04:54.180
Next, who signs what.

78
00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.255
The charter fixes the shape and
leaves the names blank for you.

79
00:05:01.255 --> 00:05:04.435
The executive sponsor,
with the CIO, owns scope,

80
00:05:04.435 --> 00:05:09.205
funding and sequence, and decides
when directory components retire.

81
00:05:09.205 --> 00:05:14.393
The steward owns the target design and the
repository, and clears the early gates.

82
00:05:14.393 --> 00:05:17.430
Workstream leads say when
their area is ready.

83
00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.854
Around them, the CISO sets
the control requirements,

84
00:05:20.854 --> 00:05:26.067
the authorizing official accepts risk,
and HR owns what the workforce data means.

85
00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:29.194
Many of these people already
hold the zero trust and

86
00:05:29.194 --> 00:05:32.760
identity governance roles
federal policy required.

87
00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.722
Bring the one-page ask, with
your four numbers filled in.

88
00:05:39.722 --> 00:05:44.338
Bring the external deadlines that apply
to your estate, candidate names for

89
00:05:44.338 --> 00:05:48.586
sponsor and steward, a proposed start
date, and a date for the first gate.

90
00:05:48.586 --> 00:05:52.223
And leave the room with one
decision recorded, whatever it is.

91
00:05:52.223 --> 00:05:54.896
To go deeper, go back
to the Start Here page,

92
00:05:54.896 --> 00:05:59.300
then Orientation and Program for
the charter and the decisions, The

93
00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.647
Transformation Arc for the roadmap, and
HR-Driven Identity for HR-driven identity.

94
00:06:04.647 --> 00:06:06.673
Every claim is footnoted there.

95
00:06:06.673 --> 00:06:08.790
Thank you for watching the Start series.
